+## D112: the two pre-session editor surfaces carry an explicit condition, and a failed load is not the pre-session state (amendment 1, R43)
+
+**Owner ruling, 2026-07-31, option A of the question Task 3's review parked.** After a profile fails to load the editor renders the "Selected profile" line and the parse error, and renders neither the empty-state paragraph nor the recents section; those two show only before anything has been opened or created at all. The loss he accepted with it, named rather than discovered later: in that one state the recents shortcut is not offered, so re-picking a profile costs a click through Open, which is in the action row in every state. The option is his and is not re-argued here. What this record settles is the gate condition his own cost clause demanded with it - the two gates need an explicit definition rather than an inherited one, "or the contradiction returns two tasks later".
+
+**This decision arrives after D107 shipped**, and it supersedes two of that record's clauses in part rather than editing them (`proc-supersede-never-overwrite`): decision 3f's recents gate and decision 7's empty-state gate both keep their text, each with a pointer, because they are the record of what Task 3 built, measured and committed. Everything else in D106 to D110 stands; D107 decision 7's diagnostics half in particular is untouched, and it is the reason the failed-load state still explains itself.
+
+**Decision:**
+
+1. **The condition is one named `computed` in `EditorView.vue`, `nothingOpenedOrCreated`, read by both surfaces and by nothing else:** `!model.value && currentPath.value === null`. No gate in the view reads a bare `!model` afterwards.
+2. **Two terms, because two facts have to be absent at once.** `model` carries "the editor holds something"; `currentPath` carries "a file has been bound to the editor". A load that resolves but fails to parse leaves the second set and clears the first - `openPath` sets `currentPath`, then assigns `doc.profile ?? undefined` - which is exactly what separates that state from the pre-session one.
+3. **The condition is NOT `!sessionActive`.** `sessionActive` becomes a `computed` over `savedSnapshot`, and D108 decision 9 nulls `savedSnapshot` on a failed load, so `!sessionActive` is true in precisely the state these two surfaces must stay hidden in.
+4. **No third state flag is added.** The two existing refs already determine the fact.
+5. **D107 decision 3's duty split is preserved, not reopened.** `currentPath` is read here for the one question it answers - which file the editor is bound to - never as a stand-in for "may I edit" or "is there content", which is what that decision moved off it. The `!model` term still carries the has-content half, so a created-and-unsaved profile hides both surfaces with no path in existence.
+6. **No new catalog string, and no other gate moves.** The editing surface, the path line with its unsaved branch, and the diagnostics section keep their own conditions, because the ruling keeps the path line and the parse error rendering in the state it changes. The editor catalog budget is unaffected.
+
+**Rationale:** the fact the two surfaces need is "nothing has been opened or created at all", and the tree already holds it in two refs - one per half of the question - so the condition is a derivation rather than a new piece of state. Deriving it is also what makes it survive a funnel nobody has written yet: any funnel that puts content into the editor writes `model`, and any that binds a file writes `currentPath`, so D107-i's derivation-package funnel lands at "model set, path null" and both surfaces hide with nothing added for it. The failed-load state is the one the ruling turns on, and the ordering inside `openPath` is what makes it identifiable: the path is set before the model is cleared, and both are synchronous statements after the awaited load, so no render falls between them and there is no frame in which the condition is momentarily true. In the other direction, `createBlank` clears `currentPath` and assigns the seed in one synchronous body, so clearing the path can never flash the empty state back. What the condition buys, in the state that produced the ruling: the path line names the failing file, the panel carries the parse error, and neither a second sentence saying no profile is open nor a recents list offering a fresh start contradicts what is already on screen. The measurement behind the option's shape is D107's own, restated because this decision rests on it: the rendered error is `parse-error = The profile could not be parsed: { $detail }` and carries a detail, not a path, so the "Selected profile" line is the only place the failing file is named.
+
+**What this leaves uncovered, stated rather than implied.** The single-definition property is pinned by a one-shot check at the implementing task's verification, not by a standing guard: nothing in the tree turns red if a later round re-inlines a bare gate into one of the two surfaces, and the honest catch for that is the diff review. The check also matches one exact spelling of the gate attribute, so a gate written with different spacing would escape it; no gate in this file is written that way, which is what its pre-state run measures rather than assumes.
+
+**Rejected alternatives:**
+
+- **Gate both surfaces on `!sessionActive`.** Steelman, at strength: it is the term this package already coined for "a profile entered the editor through one of its own funnels", it is one term instead of two, and read against the tree as Task 3 left it it is exactly right - `sessionActive` is a ref there, set by both funnels and never cleared, so it is true after a failed open and both surfaces would hide correctly. Rejected because the next task redefines it: once `sessionActive` derives from `savedSnapshot`, a failed load makes it false and the gate inverts in the one state it was written for. That is the contradiction the ruling's cost clause predicted, landing two tasks after the sentence that caused it.
+- **A dedicated ref set by both funnels and never cleared** (`hasOpenedOrCreated`). Steelman: it names the fact in one word instead of deriving it from two, no later change to `model` or `currentPath` can perturb it, and unlike `sessionActive` nothing else would consume it, so no other task could redefine it out from under this gate. Rejected because it is a third state flag carrying information the two existing refs already determine, and because it has to be remembered: any future funnel that puts content into the editor must set it, and the one that forgets leaves the empty state rendering over a populated editor. The derived condition needs no such memory - the same reasoning D108 decision 4 applies to the save state, one surface down.
+- **Clear `currentPath` on a failed load and leave both gates on `!model`.** Steelman: one assignment changes instead of two gates, no new term is introduced at all, and "the editor is bound to a file" arguably ought to mean the file actually parsed. Rejected because it deletes the only place the failing file is named, which is the measurement that decided the owner's option. It reverses the ruling while appearing to implement it.
